17] [17592186050625 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "59db5455-7d67-57c4-8cb4-9c457475eed5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1033 2453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050716 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "60d65c05-8180-5d09-9673-b4401c622309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1031 "Creating Exponential Growth Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1033 1499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6e409d61-3310-5c51-abad-da7413b800ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1031 "Constructing Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1033 3721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7931e951-f0f8-5132-97e2-67fa64802a65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228877 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228877 #edb/ref 1071 "According to a recent report, $40\\%$ of viewers said they enjoyed documentaries. Of those, $50\\%$ preferred documentaries about nature. What percentage of all viewers preferred nature documentaries?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228877 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f528740-2fa6-5441-ab8b-86ce294dbf75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228877 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the total number of viewers.\n\nWe're told that $40\\%$ of viewers enjoyed documentaries. Thus, the number of viewers who enjoy documentaries is\n$0.4x$.\n\nWe are also told that of those viewers who enjoy documentaries, $50\\%$ preferred nature topics. So, the number of viewers who preferred nature documentaries is\n\n$$\n0.50(0.4x) = 0.20x\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.20x$ represents $20\\%$ of $x$. Therefore, $20\\%$ of all viewers preferred nature documentaries." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228882 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228882 #edb/ref 1071 "A service adjusted a subscription fee to $90\\%$ of its original cost. A surcharge of $18\\%$ was applied to the adjusted fee. If the subscription originally cost $\\[math]180$, what is the surcharge?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97d0ab51-97cd-5781-be30-4d70adc5d214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228882 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the adjusted fee is $90\\%$ of the original cost $(\\[math]180){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90% \\cdot 180 &= \\frac{90}{100} \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 0.9 \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 162\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the adjusted fee is $\\[/math]162$. Then, the surcharge is $18\\%$ of this fee:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18% \\cdot 162 &= \\frac{18}{100} \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 0.18 \\cdot 162 \\\\\n&= 29.16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surcharge is $\\[math]29.16$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228884 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228884 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $160\\%$ of $75$, and then $150\\%$ of the result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91ca6940-1bc7-583b-8bac-81d7da934167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228884 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find $160\\%$ of $75{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n160% \\cdot 75 &= \\frac{160}{100} \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 1.6 \\cdot 75 \\\\\n&= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $150\\%$ of the result $(120){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n150% \\cdot 120 &= \\frac{150}{100} \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 1.5 \\cdot 120 \\\\\n&= 180\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $180$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229193 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229193 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229193 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Subsequent Percentages" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229193 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229193 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ec0d62dd-5b51-5286-b382-776412570561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229194 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229194 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Subsequent Percentages in Real-World Situations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229194 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229194 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3852a0f9-f1ac-5a3f-9883-8dd2ee03e332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229195 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229195 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229195 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229195 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229195 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing Subsequent Percentages as a Single Percentage" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229195 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229195 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d35c9f72-a876-5fc0-8ab4-741c37cb3775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1045 "Applying Percentages in Succession" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186230113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229243 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "72a697c0-6ee2-52bc-82b4-b77c00f05069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230113 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230113 #edb/ref 1061 615754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e6a90ca-fd31-5cbd-a1b4-0385b456029a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230114 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230114 #edb/ref 1061 615756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230114 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b793c54c-3bb6-5cc0-a0c0-48969b4d982c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230114 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230115 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230115 #edb/ref 1061 615760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230115 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c03e2806-a10c-543b-9b5f-7884c7db45f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230115 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230116 #edb/ref 1061 615763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80cd6820-e776-5d8b-80df-989f1f917a46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230117 #edb/ref 1061 615764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230117 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dbf3edd7-461c-5d9e-b8a3-744c45503bf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230117 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1064 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150$, and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40% \\cdot 150 &= \\frac{40}{100} \\cdot 150 \\\\\n&= 0.4 \\cdot 150 \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60$.\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25% \\cdot 60 &= \\frac{25}{100} \\cdot 60 \\\\\n&= 0.25 \\cdot 60 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1065 14335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81602213-1c20-5c8a-86ba-be4dd5c94554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1064 "When percentages are applied in succession, it is useful to represent their combined effect as a single percentage of the original amount.\n\nThis means that, instead of applying one percentage and then another, we can multiply their decimal multipliers to find an overall multiplier (or percentage) for the total change.\n\nFor example, suppose that a city’s library membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Of these members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. What percentage of last year’s members borrowed digital books?\n\nFirst, let $x$ be the number of members in the previous year.\n\nWe're told that membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Thus, the new number of members is\n\n$$\n1.5x\n$$\n\nWe are also told that of those members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. So, the number of members who borrowed digital books is\n\n$$\n0.4(1.5x) = 0.6x\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.6x$ represents $60\\%$ of $x$. Therefore, $60\\%$ of last year’s members borrowed digital books." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1065 14343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7bc4fcd7-c5b4-530f-8d3e-668da5241ab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1067 "Subsequent Percentage Applications as a Single Percentage" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
